<Placemark><name>Part A: The rise of Nazi Germany</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>PART A · THE RISE OF NAZI GERMANY (1919–1939)</b></p><p><b>Big question:</b> How did Germany go from a democracy to a dictatorship in just 14 years?</p><p><b>Your timeline for Part A:</b></p><ul><li>1919: the Treaty of Versailles · the Weimar Republic begins</li><li>1923: money becomes worthless · Hitler's Beer Hall Putsch</li><li>1929: the Great Depression begins</li><li>1933: Hitler becomes chancellor · the Reichstag fire · the Enabling Act · Dachau camp opens</li><li>1935: the Nuremberg Laws</li></ul><p><b>As you travel:</b> collect clues. At the end of Part A you should be able to answer the big question.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>8.50000</longitude><latitude>50.30000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>0</tilt><range>1500000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#chapA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>8.50000,50.30000,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>1. The Treaty of Versailles · 28 June 1919</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/versailles-hall.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/versailles-signatures.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING IN:</b> the Hall of Mirrors, Palace of Versailles, France · 28 June 1919</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>World War I ended in November 1918, and Germany lost. The countries that won, called the Allies (France, Britain and the USA), decided on the peace terms. Germany was not allowed to argue about them. On 28 June 1919, German leaders signed the peace treaty in this room.</p><p>The treaty was very hard on Germany:</p><ul><li>Germany had to accept the blame for the war (Article 231, the "war guilt clause").</li><li>Germany had to pay for the damage (Article 232). In 1921 the bill was set at 132 billion gold marks.</li><li>Germany's army was cut to 100 000 soldiers, with no air force.</li><li>Germany lost about 13% of its land in Europe and all of its colonies.</li></ul><p><b>South African link:</b> Germany's colony German South West Africa (today Namibia) was handed to South Africa to govern.</p><p>Many Germans were angry. They called the treaty a "Diktat": a peace that was forced on them. Later, Hitler and the Nazis used this anger to win support.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Treaty: a written agreement between countries.</li><li>Reparations: money a country pays for the damage it caused in a war.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows the signing in this room. It is packed with people. Who do you think made the decisions here? Why do you think the winners chose a grand palace for the signing?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: cause and consequence (how one thing leads to another)</p><ol><li>On what date was the Treaty of Versailles signed? (1)</li><li>Which article of the treaty said Germany was to blame for the war? (1)</li><li>Choose TWO terms of the treaty. Explain how each one hurt Germany. (4)</li><li>Why were many Germans angry about the treaty? How could this anger help the Nazis later? (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: the signing in the Hall of Mirrors, 28 June 1919. Sources: Treaty of Versailles (1919); USHMM.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gICOm-1tNMg">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gICOm-1tNMg</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>2.12040</longitude><latitude>48.80490</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.12040,48.80490,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>2. The Weimar Republic · 1919–1933</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Tafel-Weimarer_r.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the German National Theatre, Weimar, Germany · 1919–1933</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>After World War I, the German emperor (the Kaiser) gave up power. On 9 November 1918 Germany became a republic: a country with no king or emperor. Berlin was dangerous because of street fighting. So in 1919 the new leaders met here, in this theatre in the quiet town of Weimar. They wrote new rules for the country, called a constitution. That is why this time is called the Weimar Republic.</p><p>Germany was now a democracy. All adults, including women, could vote for the parliament, called the Reichstag.</p><p>But the new democracy had big problems:</p><ul><li>Too many small parties: no party could rule alone, so governments kept falling apart.</li><li>Article 48: in an emergency, the president could make laws on his own. After 1930 this happened more and more.</li><li>Violence: groups on the far left and far right tried to take over by force.</li><li>Hyperinflation, 1923: money became almost worthless. In November 1923, one US dollar cost about 4.2 trillion German marks. People's savings were wiped out.</li><li>The Great Depression, 1929: businesses closed and millions of people lost their jobs.</li></ul><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Democracy: a system where the people choose their leaders by voting.</li><li>Hyperinflation: when prices rise so fast that money loses almost all its value.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows a plaque on this theatre. It says that here the German people gave themselves the Weimar Constitution on 11 August 1919. Now read Article 48 of that constitution: "If public security and order are seriously disturbed or endangered... the President of the Reich may take measures necessary for their restoration, intervening if need be with the assistance of the armed forces." Who decides when there is an emergency? Why could this be dangerous?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: context (understanding the times people lived in)</p><ol><li>What does "hyperinflation" mean? (2)</li><li>Why did the new leaders meet in Weimar and not in Berlin? (1)</li><li>Explain TWO weaknesses of the Weimar Republic. (4)</li><li>Sort these problems into two lists, Political and Economic: governments keep falling apart; money becomes worthless; violent groups try to take over; millions lose their jobs; the president rules with emergency laws; people's savings are wiped out. (3)</li></ol><p>Photo: plaque on the German National Theatre, Weimar (Wikimedia Commons). Sources: Weimar Constitution (1919); German History in Documents and Images.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kJmbLz-5Ubg">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kJmbLz-5Ubg</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>11.32430</longitude><latitude>50.97970</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.32430,50.97970,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>3. Hitler and the Nazi Party in the 1920s · 8–9 November 1923</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/hitler-portrait.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> Rosenheimer Strasse, Munich, Germany, where the Bürgerbräukeller beer hall stood · 8–9 November 1923</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>In 1919 a small party called the German Workers' Party started in Munich. A former soldier named Adolf Hitler joined it in September 1919. In 1920 it changed its name to the National Socialist German Workers' Party: the Nazi Party. In 1921 Hitler became its leader.</p><p>The Nazis wanted:</p><ul><li>to make Germany powerful again and tear up the Treaty of Versailles</li><li>to get rid of democracy and communism</li><li>to push Jewish people out of German life. Hatred of Jewish people is called antisemitism.</li></ul><p>On the night of 8 November 1923, Hitler and armed men burst into a meeting in a beer hall that stood right here. He announced that he was taking over the government. The next day they marched through Munich, and the police opened fire. 16 Nazis and 4 policemen died. This failed attempt is called the Beer Hall Putsch.</p><p>Hitler went to prison, where he wrote a book called Mein Kampf ("My Struggle"). He was let out in December 1924, after less than a year. He then decided to win power by getting votes in elections instead of using guns. At first this did not work: in 1928 the Nazis got only 2.6% of the votes.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Putsch: a sudden attempt to take over a government by force.</li><li>Antisemitism: hatred of, or prejudice against, Jewish people.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo is a portrait of Hitler in his party uniform. Photos of leaders are usually planned carefully. What impression is this photo trying to give? Why should we question photos like this?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: chronology (putting events in time order)</p><ol><li>Put these events in the right order and write the year next to each: Hitler becomes party leader; the party is renamed the Nazi Party; the Beer Hall Putsch; Hitler joins the German Workers' Party. (4)</li><li>The putsch failed. How did it still help Hitler become famous? (2)</li><li>After prison, how did Hitler change his plan to win power? (2)</li></ol><p>Photo: portrait of Adolf Hitler. Sources: USHMM; Stibbe (2023).</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://youtu.be/J1HeWBMDx18">https://youtu.be/J1HeWBMDx18</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>11.58930</longitude><latitude>48.12960</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1400</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.58930,48.12960,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>4. The Great Depression and Hitler's appointment · 1929 – 30 January 1933</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/unemployed-queue.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING ON:</b> Wilhelmstrasse, Berlin, Germany, the street of government offices · 1929 – 30 January 1933</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>In October 1929 the stock market in New York crashed. This started the Great Depression, a crisis that hit the whole world. Germany had borrowed lots of money from the USA, and now the Americans wanted it back. German banks and factories closed. By 1932 about 5.6 million Germans had no jobs. Many families could not pay for food or rent.</p><p>People lost faith in democracy because it could not fix the crisis. More people voted for extreme parties, like the Nazis and the Communists. Hitler promised jobs, a strong government and a proud Germany. He also falsely blamed Jewish people and Communists for Germany's problems.</p><p>Look how fast the Nazi vote grew:</p><ul><li>1928: 2.6%</li><li>1930: 18.3%</li><li>July 1932: 37.3%</li><li>November 1932: 33.1%</li><li>March 1933: 43.9%</li></ul><p>By July 1932 the Nazis were the biggest party in parliament. But they never won more than half the votes in a free election. On 30 January 1933, President Paul von Hindenburg appointed Hitler as chancellor, the head of the government, in the offices on this street. Other politicians thought they could control him. They were wrong.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Great Depression: a worldwide economic crisis that began in 1929.</li><li>Chancellor: the head of the German government.</li><li>Unemployed: without a job.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows unemployed men queuing outside a job office in Hanover in the early 1930s. Look at the writing on the wall. Now use the photo and the vote numbers together: why would a man in this queue listen to Hitler's promises?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: using evidence</p><ol><li>What share of the votes did the Nazis get in July 1932? (1)</li><li>Who appointed Hitler as chancellor, and on what date? (2)</li><li>Explain how the Great Depression hurt Germany's economy. (3)</li><li>Why did more people vote for the Nazis between 1928 and 1930? (2)</li><li>"The German people elected Hitler as their leader." Is this true? Explain. Then say how important the Great Depression was in helping Hitler. (3)</li></ol><p>Photo: unemployed men in Hanover, early 1930s. Sources: USHMM, "Hitler comes to power".</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jFICRFKtAc4">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jFICRFKtAc4</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>13.38220</longitude><latitude>52.51130</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1800</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.38220,52.51130,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>5. The Reichstag fire and the Enabling Act · 27 February – 14 July 1933</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Reichstagsbrand.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Reichstag (parliament building), Berlin, Germany · 27 February – 14 July 1933</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Hitler had been chancellor for less than a month when, on the night of 27 February 1933, this building was set on fire. Hitler blamed the Communists. The very next day, an emergency law took away people's basic rights, like free speech and a free press. Thousands of Hitler's opponents were arrested.</p><p>Because the Reichstag was damaged, parliament met in an opera house across the square. On 23 March 1933 it voted for the Enabling Act. This law let Hitler make laws on his own, without parliament. Armed Nazi men stood around the hall while the members voted. The Communist members had been arrested or were hiding, so they could not vote.</p><p>Then Hitler moved fast:</p><ul><li>May 1933: trade unions were shut down.</li><li>14 July 1933: the Nazi Party became the only party allowed in Germany.</li><li>August 1934: after President Hindenburg died, Hitler made himself Führer (leader) of Germany.</li></ul><p>Germany was now a dictatorship.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Enabling Act: the law that let Hitler make laws without parliament.</li><li>Dictatorship: a country ruled by one person or party, with no free elections.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows firefighters at the burning Reichstag. The vote on the Enabling Act was 444 for and 94 against, and only the Social Democrats voted against it. On paper the vote looks fair. What clues in the text show that it was not really a free vote?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: comparison</p><ol><li>When did the Reichstag burn, and who did Hitler blame? (2)</li><li>Explain how the Reichstag fire helped Hitler get rid of his opponents. (3)</li><li>Why was the Enabling Act so important for Hitler? (3)</li><li>Give TWO differences between Germany as a democracy (the Weimar Republic) and Germany in July 1933. Think about parties, newspapers and parliament. (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: firefighters at the burning Reichstag, 27 February 1933 (US National Archives). Sources: USHMM.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gZiF32_9wCY">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gZiF32_9wCY</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>13.36900</longitude><latitude>52.51780</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1800</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.36900,52.51780,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>6. The Nuremberg Laws · 15 September 1935</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Nazi Party Rally Grounds, Nuremberg, Germany · 15 September 1935</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Every year the Nazis held huge rallies here, with marching soldiers, giant flags and searchlights. These rallies were propaganda: shows designed to make people admire the Nazis.</p><p>At the rally on 15 September 1935, the Nazis announced two new laws, called the Nuremberg Laws:</p><ul><li>The Reich Citizenship Law: only people of "German blood" could be citizens. Jewish people lost their citizenship and their right to vote.</li><li>The Law to Protect German Blood: Jewish people and non-Jewish Germans were no longer allowed to marry.</li></ul><p>The Nazis decided who was Jewish by looking at a person's grandparents, not at what the person believed. Later these laws were also used against Roma and Black people. Racism was now part of German law.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Propaganda: information used to make people believe certain ideas, often by twisting the truth.</li><li>Citizen: a person with full rights in a country, like the right to vote.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The image is a chart the Nazis made in 1935 to explain the laws. It uses circles to show a person's grandparents. Jewish Germans lost their rights step by step: 1 April 1933, Jewish shops boycotted · April 1933, Jewish government workers fired · May 1933, books burned · September 1935, the Nuremberg Laws · November 1938, Kristallnacht: synagogues and shops attacked · November 1938, Jewish children banned from German schools. Why do you think the Nazis took rights away step by step, and not all at once?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: significance (why an event matters)</p><ol><li>Name TWO rights Jewish Germans lost under the Nuremberg Laws. (2)</li><li>Explain how the Nuremberg Laws made racism part of Germany's laws. (3)</li><li>The Nazis decided who was Jewish by looking at a person's grandparents. What does this tell you about Nazi ideas about race? (2)</li><li>Why were the Nuremberg Laws an important step towards the Holocaust? (3)</li></ol><p>Image: Nazi chart explaining the Nuremberg Laws, 1935 (USHMM). Sources: USHMM, "Nuremberg Laws".</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L52e29SVyYc">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L52e29SVyYc</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>11.11370</longitude><latitude>49.43290</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>2800</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.11370,49.43290,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>7. Persecution and the first concentration camp · from 22 March 1933</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/boycott-1933.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Dachau Concentration Camp Memorial Site, near Munich, Germany · from 22 March 1933</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Dachau opened in March 1933, only weeks after Hitler came to power. It was one of the first concentration camps, and it became the model for all the others. The first prisoners were Hitler's political opponents: Communists, Social Democrats and trade union members. They were locked up without a trial.</p><p>The Nazis also persecuted:</p><ul><li>Jewish people</li><li>Roma and Sinti people</li><li>people with disabilities</li><li>Black people</li><li>Jehovah's Witnesses</li><li>gay men</li></ul><p>More than 200 000 people from over 30 countries were held at Dachau. More than 41 000 of them died here. American soldiers freed the camp on 29 April 1945.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Concentration camp: a prison camp where people were held without a trial and treated cruelly.</li><li>Persecution: treating a group of people cruelly because of who they are or what they believe.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo was taken in Berlin on 1 April 1933, the day the Nazis told Germans not to buy from Jewish shops. A Nazi SA man stands outside a Jewish-owned shop. Look at the people in the background. How are they reacting? What does this tell us about how ordinary Germans responded?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: perspective (seeing through other people's eyes)</p><ol><li>Who were the first prisoners at Dachau? (1)</li><li>Name THREE groups the Nazis persecuted. (3)</li><li>Why do you think the Nazis locked up their political opponents first? (2)</li><li>Look at the photo. What does it suggest about how ordinary Germans reacted to persecution? Use details from the photo. (3)</li></ol><p>Photo: the Nazi boycott of Jewish shops, Berlin, 1 April 1933. Sources: USHMM; Dachau Concentration Camp Memorial Site.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V6wT37-Orlo">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V6wT37-Orlo</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>11.46820</longitude><latitude>48.27000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>3000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinA</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.46820,48.27000,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: Kristallnacht · 9–10 November 1938</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: THE NEW SYNAGOGUE, BERLIN · 9–10 November 1938</b></p><p>On the night of 9 to 10 November 1938, Nazis attacked Jewish people all over Germany. They burned hundreds of synagogues, smashed the windows of about 7 500 Jewish shops and arrested about 30 000 Jewish men. Because of all the broken glass in the streets, this night is called Kristallnacht, the "Night of Broken Glass".</p><p>That night, Nazis set fire to this synagogue. A local police officer, Wilhelm Krützfeld, chased them away and called the fire brigade, so the building was saved.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Most people did nothing to stop Kristallnacht. What does the police officer's choice show about the power of one person?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>13.39430</longitude><latitude>52.52490</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.39430,52.52490,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The footprints of Part A (1919–1935)</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>THE FOOTPRINTS OF PART A</b></p><p>Follow the white line. It joins the 7 places you have just visited, in date order, from Versailles in 1919 to Dachau in the 1930s.</p><p>Look at the map: apart from Versailles, every stop is inside Germany. In just 16 years, Germany went from losing a war to being ruled by a dictator.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Which stop on this line do you think was the biggest turning point? Why?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>7.00000</longitude><latitude>50.50000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>1600000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_white</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>2.1204,48.8049,0 11.3243,50.9797,0 11.5893,48.1296,0 13.3822,52.5113,0 13.3690,52.5178,0 11.1137,49.4329,0 11.4682,48.2700,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Part B: World War II in Europe</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>PART B · WORLD WAR II IN EUROPE (1939–1945)</b></p><p><b>Big question:</b> How did people live through the war and the Holocaust, and how did some of them fight back?</p><p><b>Your timeline for Part B:</b></p><ul><li>1939: Germany invades Poland (1 September). The war begins.</li><li>1940: the Auschwitz camp opens</li><li>1942: Nazi leaders plan the murder of all the Jews in Europe</li><li>1943: Sophie Scholl is executed (22 February) · the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising (19 April – 16 May)</li><li>1945: Dietrich Bonhoeffer is executed (9 April) · Hitler dies (30 April) · Germany surrenders (8 May)</li></ul><p><b>As you travel:</b> look at the different ways people resisted the Nazis. Which took the most courage?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>17.00000</longitude><latitude>51.50000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>0</tilt><range>2200000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#chapB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>17.00000,51.50000,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>8. Lebensraum and the outbreak of war · 1 September 1939</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/deseret-news-1939.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> Westerplatte, near Gdańsk, Poland · 1 September 1939</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Hitler wanted Lebensraum, which means "living space". He wanted to take land in Eastern Europe for German people to live on. At first Britain and France gave in to some of his demands, hoping to avoid another war. This was called appeasement.</p><p>Early in the morning of 1 September 1939, a German warship opened fire on the small Polish army base here at Westerplatte. These were some of the first shots of World War II. About 200 Polish soldiers held out for seven days.</p><p>Germany attacked Poland from three sides with about 1.5 million soldiers, more than 2 000 tanks, 900 bombers and 400 fighter planes. This kind of attack was called Blitzkrieg, which means "lightning war": fast tanks and soldiers, with planes bombing from above. Poland's army could not get ready in time. On 17 September the Soviet Union attacked Poland from the east. Warsaw, the capital, gave up on 28 September 1939.</p><p>Two sides fought the war:</p><ul><li>The Axis powers: Germany, Italy and Japan.</li><li>The Allied powers: Britain and France, with countries like South Africa. The Soviet Union and the USA joined in 1941.</li></ul><p><b>South African link:</b> South Africa declared war on Germany on 6 September 1939. Thousands of South Africans fought in North Africa and Italy.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Lebensraum: "living space", the land Hitler wanted to take in Eastern Europe.</li><li>Blitzkrieg: "lightning war", a fast surprise attack with tanks and planes.</li><li>Appeasement: giving in to some demands to avoid a war.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo is the front page of an American newspaper, the Deseret News, on 1 September 1939. The headline says: "War rages on Polish front. German troops invade; bombers rain death." Which words show how fast and violent the attack was?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: chronology</p><ol><li>What does Lebensraum mean? (2)</li><li>Name TWO countries that were Allied powers in World War II. (2)</li><li>What was Blitzkrieg? Give TWO reasons why Poland was defeated so quickly. (3)</li><li>Look at the newspaper. Which words show how fast and violent the attack was? Why is a newspaper printed on that very day useful to a historian? (3)</li></ol><p>Photo: Deseret News, 1 September 1939 (Miller, 2024). Sources: USHMM, "Invasion of Poland, fall 1939"; Cartwright (2025).</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZLAqVtXph8w">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZLAqVtXph8w</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>18.67050</longitude><latitude>54.40690</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>4000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>18.67050,54.40690,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>German attack from the north · 1 September 1939</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BLITZKRIEG: THE ATTACK ON POLAND</b></p><p>The red lines show how German armies attacked Poland on 1 September 1939: from the north (East Prussia), from the west (Silesia) and from the south (Slovakia). All the lines point towards Warsaw, the capital.</p><p>On 17 September the Soviet army (the yellow line) attacked from the east. Poland was trapped between two enemies. Warsaw gave up on 28 September.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Look at the map. Why was Poland so hard to defend?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>21.50000</longitude><latitude>52.30000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>1600000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_red</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>20.5103,54.7104,0 21.0122,52.2297,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>German attack from the west · 1 September 1939</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The main German attack came from Silesia in the west and drove towards Łódź and Warsaw.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>21.50000</longitude><latitude>52.30000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>1600000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_red</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>17.0385,51.1079,0 19.4560,51.7592,0 21.0122,52.2297,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>German attack from the south · 1 September 1939</name><description><![CDATA[<p>German soldiers also attacked from Slovakia in the south, towards the city of Kraków.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>21.50000</longitude><latitude>52.30000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>1600000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_red</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>18.7394,49.2231,0 19.9450,50.0647,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Soviet attack from the east · 17 September 1939</name><description><![CDATA[<p>On 17 September 1939 the Soviet Union attacked Poland from the east. Germany and the Soviet Union had secretly agreed to split Poland between them.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>21.50000</longitude><latitude>52.30000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>1600000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_yellow</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>27.5615,53.9045,0 23.7340,52.0976,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>9. The Warsaw Ghetto and its uprising · 1940 – 16 May 1943</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Monument to the Ghetto Heroes, Warsaw, Poland · 1940 – 16 May 1943</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>In November 1940 the Germans built walls around part of Warsaw and forced more than 400 000 Jewish people to live inside. This closed-off area was called a ghetto. It was very crowded and there was little food. Tens of thousands of people died of hunger and disease.</p><p>In the summer of 1942, the Germans sent about 265 000 Jews from the ghetto to Treblinka, a death camp, where they were murdered.</p><p>When the Germans came to take the rest, the Jewish people fought back. The uprising began on 19 April 1943. About 700 young Jewish fighters, led by 24-year-old Mordecai Anielewicz, fought about 2 000 German soldiers who had tanks and big guns. The fighters held out for 27 days. The Germans burned the ghetto down, building by building, and crushed the uprising on 16 May 1943.</p><p>It was the biggest Jewish uprising of the war. This monument stands where the ghetto was.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Ghetto: a closed-off part of a city where the Nazis forced Jewish people to live.</li><li>Uprising: when people fight back against those who rule them.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows this monument. The numbers tell the story: more than 400 000 people forced into the ghetto · about 265 000 sent to their deaths in 1942 · about 700 fighters · 27 days of fighting. The fighters knew they could not win. Why do you think they still chose to fight?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: significance</p><ol><li>When did the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising start and end? (2)</li><li>Describe TWO hardships of daily life in the ghetto. (2)</li><li>The uprising failed. Use the numbers to explain why it is still remembered as an important act of resistance. (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: Monument to the Ghetto Heroes, Warsaw (Grandi, 2019). Sources: USHMM, "Warsaw Ghetto Uprising".</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fjVQgDhMuis">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fjVQgDhMuis</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>20.99300</longitude><latitude>52.24950</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1800</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>20.99300,52.24950,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>10. The Holocaust and the "Final Solution" · 1940–1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> Auschwitz-Birkenau, Oświęcim, Poland · 1940–1945</p><p><b>Please note:</b> this stop is about the murder of millions of people. Take your time.</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>The Holocaust was the planned murder of six million Jewish people by Nazi Germany and the people who helped them. In January 1942, Nazi leaders met at the Wannsee Conference. They planned what they called the "Final Solution": the murder of every Jewish person in Europe.</p><p>The Nazis built six death camps in Poland. Auschwitz-Birkenau was the biggest. It opened in 1940 as a prison for Polish prisoners and grew into a huge group of camps. From 1942 most of the people sent here were Jewish.</p><p>When the trains arrived, Nazi doctors chose who would live and who would die. Young children, old people and pregnant women were told they were going to have a shower. The "showers" were gas chambers. These people were never written down in any records, so no one knows exactly how many died. About 1.1 million people were murdered at Auschwitz, about one million of them Jewish. Polish people, Roma and Sinti people, and Soviet prisoners of war were also killed here.</p><p>Soviet soldiers freed Auschwitz on 27 January 1945. Today, 27 January is International Holocaust Remembrance Day.</p><p><b>South African link:</b> the South African Holocaust and Genocide Foundation has centres in Cape Town, Johannesburg and Durban that teach about the Holocaust and human rights.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Holocaust: the murder of six million Jewish people by Nazi Germany.</li><li>Genocide: trying to destroy a whole group of people because of who they are.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows Birkenau from above, with the gatehouse and the railway line that brought the trains in. The Nazis used gentle-sounding words like "Final Solution" and "resettlement" instead of "murder". Why do you think they chose words like these?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: using evidence</p><ol><li>Who was Adolf Hitler, and what did he want to do through the "Final Solution"? (2)</li><li>Why don't historians know exactly how many people died at Auschwitz? (2)</li><li>Why did the Nazis use words like "Final Solution" instead of "murder"? What does this tell us about them? (3)</li><li>Why is it important to remember the victims as people, and not only as numbers? (2)</li></ol><p>Photo: Auschwitz II-Birkenau (Sawicki, 2024, UNESCO). Sources: USHMM; History.com (2009).</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4wkwYPlXfs4">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4wkwYPlXfs4</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>19.17840</longitude><latitude>50.03430</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>3200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>19.17840,50.03430,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: The Wannsee Conference · 20 January 1942</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: THE VILLA AT WANNSEE, BERLIN · 20 January 1942</b></p><p>On 20 January 1942, 15 senior Nazi officials met in this lakeside villa. The meeting lasted about 90 minutes. They discussed how to carry out the "Final Solution": the murder of all the Jews in Europe. Their list counted 11 million people.</p><p>The minutes (the written notes) of the meeting survived the war. Today the villa is a museum and memorial.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why are the written notes from this meeting such important evidence for historians?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>13.16550</longitude><latitude>52.43300</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.16550,52.43300,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>11. Resistance: Sophie Scholl and the White Rose · 1942 – 22 February 1943</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Denkmal_f%C3%BCr_die_Geschwister_Scholl_und_Christoph_Probst_als_Teil_des_Wei%C3%9Fe-Rose-Mahnmals.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> Geschwister-Scholl-Platz, outside the University of Munich, Germany · 1942 – 22 February 1943</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Not all Germans supported Hitler. The White Rose was a small group of university students here in Munich, with one of their professors. The group included Sophie Scholl and her brother Hans. In 1942 and 1943 they secretly wrote and handed out six leaflets. The leaflets told Germans to stand up against the Nazis, and spoke out about the murder of Jewish people. They did not use violence, only words.</p><p>On 18 February 1943, Sophie and Hans threw leaflets into the main hall of the university. A caretaker saw them and reported them, and they were arrested. On 22 February they were put on trial and executed on the same day. Sophie was only 21.</p><p>Today, copies of the leaflets are set into the pavement outside this building, so that people remember them.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Resistance: actions taken against those in power.</li><li>Gestapo: the Nazi secret police.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows the leaflets set into the pavement here. At her trial, Sophie Scholl said: "Somebody, after all, had to make a start. What we wrote and said is also believed by many others. They just don't dare express themselves as we did." What does this tell you about how other Germans felt? Why do you think they stayed quiet?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: perspective</p><ol><li>How did the White Rose resist the Nazis? (2)</li><li>Why do you think the Nazis punished the students so harshly and so quickly? (2)</li><li>How successful was the White Rose in fighting the Nazis? Give reasons for your answer. (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: the White Rose memorial, Munich (Wikimedia Commons). Sources: LMU Munich, "The White Rose resistance group"; The National WWII Museum.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=REBpIqDHFX4">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=REBpIqDHFX4</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>11.58050</longitude><latitude>48.15080</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.58050,48.15080,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: Anne Frank's hiding place · 1942–1944</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: THE ANNE FRANK HOUSE, AMSTERDAM, THE NETHERLANDS · 1942–1944</b></p><p>Anne Frank was a Jewish girl whose family fled from Germany to the Netherlands. When Germany took over the Netherlands, Jewish people there were persecuted too. In July 1942, when Anne was 13, her family went into hiding in secret rooms behind a bookcase in this building.</p><p>They hid for two years, and Anne wrote about her life in her diary. On 4 August 1944 they were discovered and arrested. Anne died in the Bergen-Belsen camp in early 1945. She was 15. Her father Otto survived and published her diary, which has been read by millions of people.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why can a diary be such powerful evidence for a historian?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>4.88400</longitude><latitude>52.37520</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>4.88400,52.37520,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: South Africans at El Alamein · 1942</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: EL ALAMEIN, EGYPT · 1942 · SOUTH AFRICAN LINK</b></p><p>World War II was also fought in the deserts of North Africa, and thousands of South Africans fought here. In June 1942 the German and Italian army captured the port of Tobruk in Libya, and about 10 000 South African soldiers were taken prisoner.</p><p>The Allies stopped the German advance here at El Alamein, about 100 km from the city of Alexandria. In October and November 1942 they won a major battle here, and South African soldiers took part. It was a turning point: after El Alamein, the Germans and Italians were pushed out of North Africa. Many soldiers who died here, including South Africans, are buried in this war cemetery.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why do you think South Africa sent soldiers to fight so far from home?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>28.94780</longitude><latitude>30.83820</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>3000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>28.94780,30.83820,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: The Battle of Stalingrad · 1942–1943</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: STALINGRAD (TODAY VOLGOGRAD), RUSSIA · 1942–1943</b></p><p>In June 1941 Germany invaded the Soviet Union. In August 1942 German armies attacked the city of Stalingrad on the River Volga. The fighting went from street to street and house to house, through a terrible winter.</p><p>The Soviet army surrounded the Germans, and on 2 February 1943 the last German soldiers surrendered. About 90 000 were taken prisoner. Nearly two million soldiers and civilians were killed, wounded or captured in the battle. After Stalingrad, Germany was mostly retreating in the east. The huge statue on this hill, The Motherland Calls, remembers the battle.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why do historians call Stalingrad a turning point of the war?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>44.53730</longitude><latitude>48.74240</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1800</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.53730,48.74240,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>12. Resistance: Dietrich Bonhoeffer and the Confessing Church · 1934 – 9 April 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1987-074-16,_Dietrich_Bonhoeffer.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Flossenbürg Concentration Camp Memorial, Germany · 1934 – 9 April 1945</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Dietrich Bonhoeffer was a German pastor (a church minister) and teacher. In 1933, soon after Hitler came to power, he spoke out against the way the Nazis treated Jewish people.</p><p>The Nazis wanted to control the churches in Germany. In 1934 Bonhoeffer helped to start the Confessing Church: a group of Christians who refused to let the Nazis take over their church. He also trained young pastors to keep the church free from Nazi control.</p><p>During the war, Bonhoeffer secretly joined a group that planned to remove Hitler from power. He also helped some Jewish people escape to Switzerland. In April 1943 the Gestapo arrested him. He was hanged here at Flossenbürg camp on 9 April 1945, just two weeks before American soldiers freed the camp.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Pastor: a minister who leads a church.</li><li>Confessing Church: Christians who refused to let the Nazis control their church.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows Bonhoeffer in 1939. Compare three kinds of resistance you have seen on this tour:</p><ul><li>The White Rose: students · leaflets · 1942–1943 · leaders executed</li><li>Bonhoeffer: a pastor · church protest, plots against Hitler and helping Jews escape · 1933–1943 · executed in 1945</li><li>The Warsaw Ghetto: young Jewish fighters · an armed uprising · April–May 1943 · the ghetto destroyed</li></ul><p>What did each of these people risk?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: comparison</p><ol><li>What was the Confessing Church, and why was it started? (2)</li><li>Give ONE way the White Rose and Bonhoeffer were the same, and ONE way they were different. (4)</li><li>Which kind of resistance do you think took the most courage: the White Rose, Bonhoeffer or the Warsaw Ghetto fighters? There is no single right answer. Use evidence to explain your choice. (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: Dietrich Bonhoeffer, 1939 (Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1987-074-16). Sources: USHMM, "Dietrich Bonhoeffer".</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vvXHJM61f94">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vvXHJM61f94</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>12.35530</longitude><latitude>49.73530</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>2400</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.35530,49.73530,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: D-Day, Omaha Beach · 6 June 1944</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: OMAHA BEACH, NORMANDY, FRANCE · 6 June 1944</b></p><p>On 6 June 1944, called D-Day, about 156 000 American, British, Canadian and other Allied soldiers landed on five beaches here in Normandy. It was the biggest attack from the sea in history. This beach, code-named Omaha, saw some of the worst fighting: German guns on the cliffs fired down on the soldiers as they came ashore.</p><p>By the end of the day the Allies had a foothold in France. Now Germany had to fight the Soviet Union in the east and the Allies in the west at the same time. Less than a year later, Germany was defeated.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why was it so hard for Germany to fight on two sides at once?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>-0.88100</longitude><latitude>49.37000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>3000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.88100,49.37000,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: Hitler's bunker · 30 April 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: THE FÜHRERBUNKER, BERLIN · 30 April 1945</b></p><p>Under the ground here was Hitler's bunker: concrete rooms built beneath the garden of his offices. Hitler moved into it in January 1945 as Soviet armies came closer to Berlin.</p><p>On 30 April 1945, with Soviet soldiers only a few streets away, he killed himself here. Today there is only a car park and an information board, so that the place does not become a shrine for people who admire Hitler.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why do you think Germany chose not to build a memorial here?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>13.38150</longitude><latitude>52.51250</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.38150,52.51250,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>13. The end of the war in Europe · 30 April – 8 May 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Museum Berlin-Karlshorst, Berlin, Germany · 30 April – 8 May 1945</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>From 1943 Germany started losing the war. The Soviet army pushed in from the east. On 6 June 1944, called D-Day, British, American and other Allied soldiers landed in France and pushed in from the west.</p><p>In April 1945 Soviet soldiers surrounded Berlin. On 25 April, Soviet and American soldiers met at the town of Torgau, cutting Germany in two. As the fighting reached the centre of Berlin, Hitler killed himself in his underground bunker on 30 April 1945.</p><p>On 7 May 1945, the German general Alfred Jodl signed Germany's surrender in Reims, France. The Soviet Union wanted it signed in Berlin too. So on 8 May 1945, Field Marshal Wilhelm Keitel signed it again, here in this building. 8 May is called VE Day: Victory in Europe Day. Russia celebrates it on 9 May.</p><p>Europe was in ruins, and millions of people had lost their homes. From November 1945, Nazi leaders were put on trial for their crimes in the city of Nuremberg.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Surrender: to give up and stop fighting.</li><li>VE Day: Victory in Europe Day, 8 May 1945.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows Soviet soldiers raising their flag over the Reichstag in Berlin in May 1945. The last weeks of the war: 16 April, the battle for Berlin begins · 25 April, Soviet and US soldiers meet at Torgau · 30 April, Hitler dies · 7 May, the surrender is signed in France · 8 May, the surrender is signed in Berlin: VE Day. Why do you think the Soviet Union wanted the surrender signed again in Berlin?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: cause and consequence</p><ol><li>On what date did the war in Europe end? (1)</li><li>Explain how TWO important events led to Germany's defeat. (3)</li><li>Why are there two different dates for VE Day? (2)</li><li>Name TWO results of the war for Europe. (2)</li></ol><p>Photo: Soviet soldiers raise a flag over the Reichstag, May 1945 (USHMM, 2022). Sources: USHMM, "World War II in Eastern Europe, 1942–1945".</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v1QnZDY-YiM">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v1QnZDY-YiM</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>13.52910</longitude><latitude>52.48620</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1400</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinB</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.52910,52.48620,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: The Nuremberg trials · 1945–1946</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: THE PALACE OF JUSTICE, NUREMBERG · 1945–1946</b></p><p>After the war, the Allies put 22 of the most important Nazi leaders on trial in Courtroom 600 of this building. The trial began on 20 November 1945. For the first time, leaders of a country were judged by an international court for war crimes and crimes against humanity.</p><p>Films and documents from the camps were shown as evidence. In October 1946, 12 of the men were sentenced to death and others were sent to prison. Three were found not guilty.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why was it important that the Nazi leaders got a trial, instead of just being punished?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>11.04800</longitude><latitude>49.45550</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1200</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.04800,49.45550,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The footprints of Part B (1939–1945)</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>THE FOOTPRINTS OF PART B</b></p><p>Follow the red line from Westerplatte, where the war began in 1939, to Berlin, where it ended in 1945.</p><p>On the way it passes places of terrible suffering (Warsaw and Auschwitz) and places of courage (Munich and Flossenbürg).</p><p><b>Think:</b> The war started in Poland and ended in Berlin. What does this tell you about how the war turned around?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>15.50000</longitude><latitude>51.00000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>1700000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_red</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>18.6705,54.4069,0 20.9930,52.2495,0 19.1784,50.0343,0 11.5805,48.1508,0 12.3553,49.7353,0 13.5291,52.4862,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Part C: World War II in the Pacific</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>PART C · WORLD WAR II IN THE PACIFIC (1931–1945)</b></p><p><b>Big question:</b> How did the war spread around the world, and how did it finally end?</p><p><b>Your timeline for Part C:</b></p><ul><li>1931: Japan invades Manchuria in China</li><li>1937: Japan goes to war with China</li><li>1941: the USA stops selling oil to Japan (July) · Japan attacks Pearl Harbor (7 December)</li><li>1945: the first atomic bomb test (16 July) · Hiroshima (6 August) · Nagasaki (9 August) · Japan surrenders (2 September)</li></ul><p><b>As you travel:</b> think about whether the atomic bombs had to be used. Historians still disagree.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>168.50000</longitude><latitude>30.00000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>0</tilt><range>9000000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#chapC</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>168.50000,30.00000,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>14. Japanese expansion and Pearl Harbor · 7 December 1941</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Pearl_Harbor_Attack,_7_December_1941_-_80-G-19942.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the USS Arizona Memorial, Pearl Harbor, Hawaii, USA · 7 December 1941</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>Japan wanted to build an empire in Asia and the Pacific. It needed oil, rubber and metals for its factories and its army. Japan invaded Manchuria, in northern China, in 1931 and started a full war with China in 1937. In 1941 the USA stopped selling oil to Japan, to try to stop it.</p><p>On the morning of 7 December 1941, Japanese planes made a surprise attack on the American navy here at Pearl Harbor. About 2 400 Americans were killed. The battleship USS Arizona blew up and sank with 1 177 men on board. The ship still lies under the water, beneath the white memorial in front of you.</p><p>The next day, 8 December, the USA declared war on Japan. On 11 December, Germany and Italy declared war on the USA. Now the war was being fought all over the world.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Empire: a group of countries or lands ruled by one powerful country.</li><li>Embargo: when a country stops trading something with another country.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows the USS Arizona burning after the attack. Japan's road to war: 1931, Manchuria invaded · 1937, war with China · 1940, Japan moves into French Indochina (today Vietnam) · July 1941, the USA stops selling oil to Japan · 7 December 1941, Pearl Harbor · February 1942, Japan captures Singapore. Why would losing its oil push Japan towards war, and not towards peace?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: cause and consequence</p><ol><li>On what date did Japan attack Pearl Harbor? Which ship still lies under the memorial? (2)</li><li>Give ONE long-term cause and ONE short-term cause of the attack. (2)</li><li>How did the attack on Pearl Harbor change the war for the USA and for Germany? (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: USS Arizona burning, 7 December 1941 (US Navy). Sources: USHMM; The National WWII Museum.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uSDZsOcCltg">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uSDZsOcCltg</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>-157.94970</longitude><latitude>21.36490</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>3000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinC</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-157.94970,21.36490,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The route of the Japanese attack · 7 December 1941</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>THE ROUTE OF THE ATTACK · 7 December 1941</b></p><p>Six Japanese aircraft carriers sailed secretly across the Pacific Ocean for about 12 days. Early on 7 December 1941, about 370 km north of Hawaii, they launched 353 planes in two waves. The planes flew south along this line to Pearl Harbor. The attack lasted about two hours.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why do you think the Japanese ships stayed so far away from Hawaii?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>-157.90000</longitude><latitude>23.00000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>900000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_red</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>-157.9000,24.8000,0 -157.9497,21.3649,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: Iwo Jima · February–March 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: MOUNT SURIBACHI, IWO JIMA, JAPAN · February–March 1945</b></p><p>Iwo Jima is a small volcanic island about 1 200 km south of Tokyo. The USA wanted it as a base for its planes. On 19 February 1945 about 70 000 US Marines landed. More than 20 000 Japanese soldiers defended the island from tunnels and caves.</p><p>On 23 February, US Marines raised the American flag on top of Mount Suribachi, the volcano here. A photo of that moment became one of the most famous photos of the war. The fighting lasted five weeks. Almost all the Japanese defenders and nearly 7 000 Americans were killed.</p><p><b>Think:</b> The battle of Iwo Jima made US leaders worry about invading Japan itself. Why?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>141.28950</longitude><latitude>24.75100</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>8000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>141.28950,24.75100,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>15. The atomic bombings · 6 and 9 August 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/nagasaki-ruins-1945.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/trinity-test-1945.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> the Atomic Bomb Dome, Hiroshima, Japan · 6 and 9 August 1945</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>By 1945 Japan was losing the war, but its leaders refused to give up. In the battles of Iwo Jima and Okinawa, Japanese soldiers fought to the last man, and many soldiers on both sides died. Harry Truman became the US president in April 1945. He wanted to end the war without invading Japan, which could have cost many more lives.</p><p>On 16 July 1945, the USA secretly tested the world's first atomic bomb in the New Mexico desert. On 26 July, the USA, Britain and China warned Japan to surrender or face "prompt and utter destruction". Japan did not surrender.</p><ul><li>6 August 1945, 08:15: the American bomber Enola Gay dropped an atomic bomb called "Little Boy" on Hiroshima. By the end of 1945, about 140 000 people had died.</li><li>8 August: the Soviet Union declared war on Japan.</li><li>9 August: a second bomb, "Fat Man", was meant for the city of Kokura, but clouds covered it. The plane bombed Nagasaki instead. By the end of 1945, about 70 000 to 74 000 people had died there.</li></ul><p>Many survivors, called hibakusha, suffered terrible burns and radiation sickness for years. The ruined building in front of you, the Atomic Bomb Dome, was left standing as a reminder.</p><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>Atomic bomb: a very powerful bomb that can destroy a whole city with its blast, heat and radiation.</li><li>Radiation sickness: illness caused by the invisible rays an atomic bomb gives off.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows Nagasaki after the bomb. Historians disagree about the bombs. Some say the bombs ended the war quickly and saved the lives that an invasion would have cost. Others say Japan was close to giving up anyway, and that killing so many ordinary people can never be right. Why do you think different books give different numbers of deaths?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: interpretation (weighing up different views)</p><ol><li>Name the plane and the bomb used on Hiroshima, and give the date. (3)</li><li>Give TWO reasons why the USA decided to use the atomic bomb. (2)</li><li>Japan did not give up straight after Hiroshima. What else happened between 6 and 15 August that put pressure on Japan? (2)</li><li>Explain the two different views about the bombs. Which view do you agree with more? Use evidence. (4)</li></ol><p>Photo: Nagasaki after the atomic bomb (CBC News). Sources: Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum; US Air Force Nuclear Weapons Center.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GVPQBr4Hqc4">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GVPQBr4Hqc4</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>132.45360</longitude><latitude>34.39550</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>1800</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinC</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>132.45360,34.39550,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The flight of the Enola Gay · 6 August 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>THE FLIGHT OF THE ENOLA GAY · 6 August 1945</b></p><p>The Enola Gay took off from the island of Tinian in the early hours of the morning. It flew about 2 400 km north, past the island of Iwo Jima, to Hiroshima. The flight took about six and a half hours. The bomb was dropped at 08:15.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why did the USA need to capture islands like Tinian and Iwo Jima before it could bomb Japan?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>136.50000</longitude><latitude>25.50000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>4200000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_orange</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>145.6340,15.0786,0 141.3200,24.7800,0 132.4536,34.3955,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The flight of Bockscar · 9 August 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Three days later the bomber Bockscar flew towards Kokura. Smoke and clouds hid the city, so after three tries the crew turned to Nagasaki and dropped the bomb at 11:02. The plane was so low on fuel that it had to make an emergency landing on Okinawa.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>136.50000</longitude><latitude>25.50000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>4200000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_yellow</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>145.6340,15.0786,0 130.8750,33.8840,0 129.8634,32.7737,0 127.7500,26.4000,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bonus stop: Nagasaki · 9 August 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>BONUS STOP: THE HYPOCENTER, NAGASAKI, JAPAN · 9 August 1945</b></p><p>At 11:02 on 9 August 1945, the atomic bomb "Fat Man" exploded about 500 metres above this spot. A black stone column now marks the hypocenter, the point on the ground right below the explosion.</p><p>The hills around Nagasaki blocked some of the blast, but much of the city was destroyed. By the end of 1945, about 70 000 to 74 000 people had died. Every year on 9 August, the people of Nagasaki stop at 11:02 to remember.</p><p><b>Think:</b> Why do you think the people of Nagasaki keep remembering the bombing every year?</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>129.86340</longitude><latitude>32.77370</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>60</tilt><range>1600</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>129.86340,32.77370,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>16. Japan surrenders: the war ends · 2 September 1945</name><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/1111kaykay1111/footprints-of-war/claude/blissful-meitner-flgv9r/img/missouri-surrender-1945.jpg" width="400" style="max-width:100%" /></p><p><b>YOU ARE STANDING AT:</b> Tokyo Bay, Japan · 2 September 1945</p><p><b>WHAT HAPPENED HERE</b></p><p>On 15 August 1945, Emperor Hirohito told the Japanese people on the radio that Japan would surrender. Most Japanese people had never heard his voice before.</p><p>On 2 September 1945, Japanese leaders signed the surrender on the deck of the American battleship USS Missouri, here in Tokyo Bay. General Douglas MacArthur accepted it for the Allies. World War II was over, six years and one day after it began.</p><p>Japan gave up for more than one reason: the two atomic bombs, the Soviet Union joining the war, and the fact that Japan was clearly losing.</p><p>World War II was the deadliest war in history. About 70 to 85 million people died, and most of them were ordinary people, not soldiers. The war still shapes the world you live in today:</p><ul><li>The United Nations was started in October 1945 to try to stop future wars.</li><li>The USA and the Soviet Union became superpowers, and the Cold War began.</li><li>The horror of the Holocaust led to the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in 1948.</li></ul><p><b>WORDS TO KNOW</b></p><ul><li>United Nations: an organisation of countries that works for peace in the world.</li><li>Human rights: the basic rights every person has, like the right to life and to be treated fairly.</li></ul><p><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b></p><p>The photo shows Japan's foreign minister, Mamoru Shigemitsu, signing the surrender on the USS Missouri while Allied officers watch. Most of the people who died in World War II were ordinary people, not soldiers. What does this tell you about how this war was fought?</p><p><b>WATCH</b> the video for this stop.</p><p><b>YOUR TASKS</b> · Historian's skill: significance</p><ol><li>Where and when did Japan sign the surrender? (2)</li><li>Explain why Japan surrendered. Give more than one reason. (3)</li><li>Name TWO ways World War II still affects the world today. (2)</li><li>Think back to Stop 1, Versailles. How was the end of World War II different from the end of World War I? (3)</li></ol><p>Photo: the surrender on USS Missouri, 2 September 1945 (Truman Library Institute). Sources: Truman Library Institute; USHMM.</p><p><b>&#9654; Video:</b> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INFQXq5trDM">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=INFQXq5trDM</a></p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>139.74000</longitude><latitude>35.34800</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>62</tilt><range>16000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#pinC</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>139.74000,35.34800,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The footprints of Part C (1941–1945)</name><description><![CDATA[<p><b>THE FOOTPRINTS OF PART C</b></p><p>The blue line crosses the Pacific Ocean, from Pearl Harbor (1941) to Hiroshima and Tokyo Bay (1945). That is more than 6 000 km. The war in the Pacific was fought across the biggest ocean on Earth.</p><p><b>Think:</b> How is the war in the Pacific different from the war in Europe? Look at the distances on the map.</p>]]></description><LookAt><longitude>168.50000</longitude><latitude>30.00000</latitude><altitude>0</altitude><heading>0</heading><tilt>20</tilt><range>9000000</range><altitudeMode>relativeToGround</altitudeMode></LookAt><styleUrl>#line_blue</styleUrl><LineString><tessellate>1</tessellate><coordinates>-157.9497,21.3649,0 132.4536,34.3955,0 139.7400,35.3480,0</coordinates></LineString></Placemark>
